      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle fundamentally states that there are inherent limits to the precision with which certain pairs of physical properties of a particle, known as conjugate variables, can be known simultaneously. Specifically, the more accurately you measure a particle's position ($\\Delta x$), the less accurately you can simultaneously know its momentum ($\\Delta p$), and vice versa, as described by the relation $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\ge \\hbar/2$. This is not a limitation of measurement equipment, but a fundamental property of quantum mechanics itself, arising because physical reality at the quantum level is probabilistic. Therefore, it is impossible to define both the exact position and the exact momentum of a particle like an electron at the same instant in time.",
